name: processes
boss: Daemon
flavor: I started before you logged in. I'll be here after.
brief: Every running program is a process with a number. ps lists them, kill
brief: sends them a signal, top watches them live.
brief:
brief: kill does not mean kill. It sends a signal, and the default is TERM,
brief: which asks politely and can be caught or ignored. KILL (9) cannot be
brief: caught, which is why it is a last resort and not a first one -- a
brief: program killed with 9 never gets to clean up after itself.
---
q: list the processes running right now
a: ps
out: PID
ref: ps
teach: ps
---
q: list processes showing just the pid and the command name
a: ps -o pid,comm
out: PID
ref: ps
teach: ps -o
---
q: list processes including their threads
a: ps -T
out: PID
ref: ps
teach: ps -T
---
q: watch processes live, updating as they change
a: top
ref: top
teach: top
hint: q leaves it
---
q: take a single snapshot of top rather than an interactive display
a: top -b -n 1
out: PID|Mem
ref: top
teach: top -b
teach: top -n
hint: b for batch mode -- plain text, no cursor tricks
---
q: run top updating every 5 seconds instead of the default
a: top -d 5
ref: top
teach: top -d
---
q: find the process ids of anything called @proc@
a: pgrep @proc@
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep
---
q: find @proc@ matching against the whole command line, not just the name
a: pgrep -f @proc@
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -f
---
q: list @proc@ processes showing the name beside each pid
a: pgrep -l @proc@
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -l
---
q: find only the newest process called @proc@
a: pgrep -n @proc@
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -n
---
q: find only the oldest process called @proc@
a: pgrep -o @proc@
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -o
---
q: find processes whose name is exactly @proc@ and not merely contains it
a: pgrep -x @proc@
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -x
---
q: find every process that is NOT called @proc@
a: pgrep -v @proc@
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -v
---
q: find the children of process 1
a: pgrep -P 1
out: [0-9]
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -P
---
q: politely ask process 12345 to exit
a: kill 12345
ref: kill
teach: kill
hint: no flag means TERM, which the program may catch
---
q: force process 12345 to die immediately, uncatchably
a: kill -9 12345
ref: kill
teach: kill -9
---
q: send process 12345 the TERM signal by number
a: kill -15 12345
ref: kill
teach: kill -15
---
q: list the signal names kill can send
a: kill -l
out: KILL
ref: kill
teach: kill -l
---
q: kill every process called @proc@ by name
a: pkill @proc@
ref: pkill
teach: pkill
---
q: force-kill every process whose full command line mentions @proc@
a: pkill -9 -f @proc@
ref: pkill
teach: pkill -f
---
q: show how much memory this machine has, in a human readable form
a: free -h
out: Mem
ref: free
teach: free -h
---
q: show memory in megabytes
a: free -m
out: Mem
ref: free
teach: free -m
---
q: show memory in kilobytes
a: free -k
out: Mem
ref: free
teach: free -k
---
q: show memory in raw bytes
a: free -b
out: Mem
ref: free
teach: free -b
---
q: how many processors does this machine have
a: nproc
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: nproc
teach: nproc
---
q: how many processors exist, including ones not available to you
a: nproc --all
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: nproc
teach: nproc --all
---
q: list @proc@ processes showing the full command line of each
a: pgrep -a @proc@
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -a
